Article 6 Where the infringed party requests compensation in accordance with Article 65 of the Tort Liability Law, it shall provide evidentiary materials proving the following facts:
(1) the polluter discharged pollutants;
(2) the infringed party suffered damage;
(3) there is a correlation between the pollutants discharged by the polluter or the secondary pollutants thereof and the damage.

Article 7 Where the polluter produces evidence to prove any of the following circumstances, the People's Court shall find that there is no causal relationship between the pollution act and the damage:
(1) the discharged pollutants could not possibly have caused the damage;
(2) the discharged pollutants that could cause the damage did not reach the place where the damage occurred;
(3) the damage had already occurred before the discharge of pollutants;
(4) other circumstances under which it may be found that there is no causal relationship between the pollution act and the damage.

Article 16 Any of the following circumstances shall be deemed as fraudulent conduct as specified in Article 65 of the Environmental Protection Law of the People's Republic of China:
(1) an environmental impact assessment institution, knowing that the materials provided by the client are false, issues a seriously inaccurate evaluation document;
(2) an environmental monitoring institution or an institution engaged in the maintenance and operation of environmental monitoring equipment intentionally conceals the fact that the client exceeds the pollutant discharge standards or exceeds the total emission control targets for key pollutants;
(3) an institution engaged in the maintenance and operation of pollution prevention facilities intentionally fails to operate or improperly operates environmental monitoring equipment or pollution prevention facilities;
(4) other fraudulent conduct by relevant institutions in environmental service activities.
